From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 14:38:22 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] uml: let a write-only mapping be read, as native
 architectures do

UML refused a load fault on a mapping that had VM_WRITE but neither
VM_READ nor VM_EXEC, and delivered SIGSEGV with SEGV_ACCERR. Native
arm64 and x86 cannot map a page writable but not readable, so write
implies read there: arm64's fault handler accepts VM_WRITE for a read,
and x86's access_error() accepts any of VM_READ, VM_WRITE and VM_EXEC.
An Android application that maps a file PROT_WRITE | MAP_SHARED and
then reads it crashed in UML and runs on a native kernel.

The load branch of handle_page_fault_access() now asks
vma_is_accessible(), the test x86 uses. The same function resolves the
kernel's own uaccess faults, so write() from such a mapping works too.
An execute-only mapping stays readable, as on arm64 without EPAN and
on x86; PROT_NONE and a store to a read-only mapping still fault.
UML's page protections already give a write-only mapping the user bit
that the host mapping reads from, so no PTE changes are needed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/um/kernel/trap.c | 11 +++++++++--
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/um/kernel/trap.c b/arch/um/kernel/trap.c
index 64a1006b06a4fcdee6a3749092d0f81a1b01b989..4f6054efa435cad2ef29cb147806058cff96109e 100644
--- a/arch/um/kernel/trap.c
+++ b/arch/um/kernel/trap.c
@@ -189,8 +189,15 @@ static int handle_page_fault_access(unsigned long address, unsigned long ip,
 			goto out;
 		flags |= FAULT_FLAG_INSTRUCTION;
 	} else {
-		/* Don't require VM_READ|VM_EXEC for write faults! */
-		if (!(vma->vm_flags & (VM_READ | VM_EXEC)))
+		/*
+		 * A load needs a mapping that permits any access, as on native
+		 * arm64 and x86: neither can map a page writable but not
+		 * readable, so write implies read.  An execute-only mapping
+		 * reads too, as on arm64 without EPAN and on x86.  Only
+		 * PROT_NONE refuses.  Every UML PTE of such a mapping already
+		 * carries the user bit, which is what the host mapping reads.
+		 */
+		if (!vma_is_accessible(vma))
 			goto out;
 	}
 
